---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import TrustBadge from "../components/TrustBadge.astro";
import SocialProof from "../components/SocialProof.astro";

const title = "Horizon 7.0 AT Treadmill Review (2026): Built Around One Idea";
const description =
  "The 7.0 AT exists because changing speed on a treadmill is slow. Its dial controls and fast-responding motor solve that, and the rest of the machine follows from it.";

const PRODUCTS = [
  {
    id: "horizon-7-0-at",
    name: "Horizon 7.0 AT",
    category: "Best Treadmill for Interval Training Under $1,200",
    badge: "Interval Pick",
    amazonUrl: "https://www.amazon.com/s?k=Horizon%207.0%20AT+Treadmill&tag=sktiger-20",
    reviewUrl: null,
    priceRange: "$999-$1,299",
    specs: "60 x 20 in. deck · 3.0 CHP · 0-15% incline · 0-12 mph · 325 lb capacity",
    pros: [
      "Dial controls on the handrails change speed and incline without breaking stride",
      "Motor is tuned to reach a new speed quickly, which is what makes short intervals workable",
      "No subscription and no account — it works with third-party apps or with nothing at all",
      "60-inch deck and 325 lb capacity are genuinely generous at this price",
      "Lifetime frame and motor warranty, as published by Horizon",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Console is deliberately basic — a small display, not a screen, and that is the trade",
      "Bluetooth speakers are adequate rather than good",
      "Heavy, and the fold reclaims floor area rather than making it portable",
    ],
    bottomLine: "The best interval treadmill under about $1,200, and a perfectly good general treadmill besides. Buy it if you do structured sessions; look elsewhere if you want a screen.",
    description: "The 7.0 AT is built around a 3.0 CHP motor driving a 20 by 60-inch belt, with motorised incline to 15% and a top speed of 12 mph, on a frame rated to 325 lbs. Horizon publishes a lifetime warranty on the frame and motor, and the deck folds with hydraulic assistance. The defining feature is a pair of dials on the handrails that change speed and incline directly, paired with a motor tuned to reach the new speed quickly rather than ramping gently. The console is a simple backlit display with Bluetooth speakers and a device shelf, and nothing on it is gated behind a membership — the machine is designed on the assumption that your training content comes from your own phone.",
  },
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 1, name: "Home", item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/" },
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 2, name: "Reviews", item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/reviews/" },
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 3, name: "Horizon 7.0 AT", item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/horizon-7-0-at-treadmill/" },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What makes the Horizon 7.0 AT good for interval training?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Two things working together. Dials sit on the handrails where your hands already are, so you can go straight to a target speed without hunting for a button or breaking stride. And the motor is tuned to reach the new speed quickly rather than ramping gently. Fast controls on a slow-ramping belt would solve nothing — it is the combination that lets a thirty-second effort actually be thirty seconds at the right pace.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Does the Horizon 7.0 AT need a subscription?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "No. There is no membership, account or trial required, and the console works permanently on its own. The machine is designed to sit alongside whatever app you already use rather than to sell you one. What you give up is an integrated screen experience — there is no instructor and no automatic speed control from a class, so you change the speed yourself when told.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What size is the Horizon 7.0 AT deck?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Horizon publishes 60 by 20 inches. Sixty inches is the threshold at which a treadmill stops constraining an average-height runner's stride, and a good number of machines at this price stop at 55, so it is one of the stronger reasons to choose it. Taller runners should still check the stride arithmetic before committing.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Horizon 7.0 AT vs Sole F63 — which is better?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "They are close on hardware and differ in philosophy. Sole builds a heavier, plainer machine with a long warranty and no dial controls. The 7.0 AT matches it broadly on specification and adds fast speed changes. If your sessions involve changing pace repeatedly, the Horizon is clearly better for you. If you run at one speed and want the most solid frame for the money, the Sole is the stronger buy.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Is the Horizon 7.0 AT good for walking?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "It works perfectly well, but it is more machine than a walker needs and almost everything you are paying for — deck length, motor headroom, fast speed changes — is irrelevant at 3 mph. Horizon's own T101 does the walking job for considerably less money with the same subscription-free approach. Buy the 7.0 AT for running and intervals, not for walking.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Does the Horizon 7.0 AT fold?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Yes, with hydraulic assistance, which matters more than the folded dimensions. An assisted fold keeps happening indefinitely, whereas a fold requiring you to take the deck's full weight tends to stop happening within a few weeks. It is still a heavy machine — folding reclaims floor area rather than making it portable, and moving it between floors is a planned event.",
          },
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      description: description,
      author: { "@type": "Person", name: "Morgan Reyes" },
      datePublished: "2026-09-18",
      dateModified: "2026-09-18",
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "Treadmill Reviews USA" },
    },
    ...PRODUCTS.map((product) => ({
      "@type": "Product",
      name: product.name,
      description: product.description,
      brand: { "@type": "Brand", name: product.name.split(" ")[0] },
      offers: {
        "@type": "AggregateOffer",
        priceCurrency: "USD",
        availability: "https://schema.org/InStock",
      },
    })),
  ],
};
---

<Layout
  title={`${title} | Treadmill Reviews USA`}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: "2026-09-18",
    modifiedTime: "2026-09-18",
    author: "Morgan Reyes",
    section: "Fitness Equipment",
    tags: [
      "horizon 7.0 at",
      "horizon fitness",
      "interval treadmill",
      "treadmill review",
      "no subscription treadmill",
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
  stickyCta={{ text: "See Who It Suits", link: "#who-should-buy" }}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <!-- Hero Section -->
    <div class="bg-gray-900 py-16 lg:py-24 relative overflow-hidden">
      <svg class="absolute inset-0 w-full h-full opacity-10" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg">
        <pattern id="dot-pattern-horizon70attreadmill" x="0" y="0" width="24" height="24" patternUnits="userSpaceOnUse">
          <circle cx="2" cy="2" r="1.5" fill="white"></circle>
        </pattern>
        <rect width="100%" height="100%" fill="url(#dot-pattern-horizon70attreadmill)"></rect>
      </svg>
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 relative z-10 text-center">
        <div class="flex justify-center mb-6">
          <TrustBadge />
        </div>
        <div class="flex items-center justify-center gap-3 mb-6 flex-wrap">
          <span class="bg-[#FF5A1F] text-white px-3 py-1 rounded-full text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest">
            Model Review
          </span>
          <span class="bg-green-500 text-white px-3 py-1 rounded-full text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest">
            Updated September 2026
          </span>
        </div>
        <h1 class="text-4xl lg:text-6xl font-black text-white leading-tight mb-8">
          Horizon 7.0 AT <span class="text-[#5AA9FF]">Treadmill</span>
        </h1>
        <p class="text-xl text-gray-300 max-w-2xl mx-auto leading-relaxed">
          Most treadmills are poor at interval training for one specific reason: the speed takes too long to change. Horizon built a machine around fixing that, and it is the most interesting thing in the price band because of it.
        </p>
        <div class="mt-8 flex flex-wrap justify-center gap-4">
          <a href="#the-idea" class="bg-[#FF5A1F] hover:bg-[#E64F16] text-white px-8 py-4 rounded-xl font-black no-underline transition-colors uppercase tracking-widest text-sm">
            The Idea Behind It
          </a>
          <a href="#who-should-buy" class="bg-white/10 hover:bg-white/20 text-white px-8 py-4 rounded-xl font-black no-underline transition-colors uppercase tracking-widest text-sm border border-white/20">
            Who Should Buy It
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>


    <!-- Author & Meta -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-12">
      <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-4 py-8 border-b border-gray-100">
        <img src="https://i.pravatar.cc/150?img=48" alt="Morgan Reyes" class="w-12 h-12 rounded-full" />
        <div>
          <p class="font-bold text-gray-900 leading-tight">Morgan Reyes</p>
          <p class="text-gray-500 text-sm leading-tight">Fitness Equipment Editor</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block w-px h-8 bg-gray-200"></div>
        <p class="text-gray-500 text-sm">
          Last Updated: <strong class="text-gray-700">September 18, 2026</strong>
        </p>
        <div class="ml-auto">
          <SocialProof users="3.6k" />
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="not-prose my-10 bg-blue-50 border border-blue-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
        <p class="text-sm text-gray-700 m-0 leading-relaxed">
          <strong class="text-gray-900">On the numbers below.</strong> The specifications here are
          Horizon’s published figures, cross-checked in September 2026 against the Horizon listing
          and major retailer listings, which agree. They are not our own measurements. Horizon
          revises machines within a model name between years, so confirm the motor rating, deck
          dimensions, incline range and warranty terms against the exact unit you are buying — and
          check the current street price, because this machine is discounted often.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
        <h2 class="flex items-center gap-2 text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mt-0 mb-4">
          <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="w-5 h-5 text-[#FF5A1F]" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" d="M4 6h16M4 12h16M4 18h7"></path></svg>
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#the-idea" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">1. The Idea Behind It</a>
          <a href="#what-it-is" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">2. What the Machine Actually Is</a>
          <a href="#no-subscription" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">3. No Subscription, and What That Means</a>
          <a href="#in-use" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">4. How It Behaves in Use</a>
          <a href="#folding-and-space" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">5. Folding, Weight and Space</a>
          <a href="#who-should-buy" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">6. Who Should Buy It</a>
          <a href="#ownership" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">7. Owning One</a>
          <a href="#alternatives" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">8. What to Cross-Shop</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">9. Frequently Asked Questions</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Almost every treadmill is bad at interval training, and the reason is not the motor size
          or the deck length. It is that pressing a button four times and then waiting eight
          seconds for the belt to catch up turns a thirty-second effort into something else
          entirely. This machine exists to fix that.
        </p>

        <div id="the-idea" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">1. The Idea Behind It</h2>
<p>
            Consider what a treadmill interval session actually requires. You are at 6 mph. The plan
            says thirty seconds at 9. On a conventional machine you find the speed button, press it
            repeatedly or hold it, and then wait while the belt accelerates gradually. By the time
            you are at 9 mph, a meaningful fraction of your thirty seconds has gone — and the same
            thing happens in reverse at the end, so the recovery is compromised too.
          </p>
          <p>
            Over a session of ten intervals, that is minutes of your workout spent at a pace you did
            not choose. The session you performed is not the session that was written.
          </p>
          <p>
            Horizon's answer has two halves. The first is the controls: dials on the handrails,
            where your hands already are, that let you go straight to a speed rather than stepping
            toward it. You turn one and you are at 9, without looking down, without hunting for a
            button, without breaking stride.
          </p>
          <p>
            The second is the motor tuning. The machine is set up to reach the new speed quickly
            rather than easing into it, which is the half that actually matters — fast controls
            attached to a gently ramping belt would solve nothing.
          </p>
          <p>
            That is the entire product thesis, and it is a good one, because it addresses a real
            problem that almost nobody else in this price band has bothered to address. The
            protocols in our
            <a href="/treadmill-interval-workouts/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">interval guide</a>
            are considerably easier to execute honestly on this machine than on a typical
            button-driven treadmill.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="what-it-is" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">2. What the Machine Actually Is</h2>
<p>
            Underneath the interval story, this is a well-specified mid-range home treadmill and it
            deserves to be assessed as one.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The deck is 60 inches long and 20 wide</strong>, as published, which is the
            specification that matters most for anyone who intends to run. Sixty inches is the
            threshold where a treadmill stops constraining an average-height runner's stride, and
            plenty of machines at this price stop at 55. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-belt-size-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">belt size guide</a>
            explains why that five inches matters more than almost any other number on a spec
            sheet.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The motor is rated 3.0 CHP</strong> — continuous duty, not peak. That
            distinction is the one most commonly exploited in this price band, and our
            <a href="/treadmill-horsepower-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">horsepower guide</a>
            covers why a peak figure is not a comparable number. A genuine 3.0 continuous rating is
            enough for sustained running by most home users.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Incline goes to 15% and speed to 12 mph</strong>, both of which exceed what most
            buyers will use and neither of which is a constraint.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Capacity is 325 lbs</strong>, which is generous here and a reasonable proxy for
            frame rigidity — see our
            <a href="/best-treadmills-for-heavy-people/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">capacity guide</a>
            for why the rated figure and the comfortable figure differ.
          </p>
          <p>
            Put together, that is a machine that would be worth cross-shopping in our
            <a href="/best-treadmills-under-2000/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">under-$2,000 field</a>
            on its hardware alone, sitting some way below that price. The interval controls are what
            it is known for, but the deck length is arguably the better reason to buy it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="no-subscription" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">3. No Subscription, and What That Means</h2>
<p>
            The 7.0 AT does not require a membership, an account or a trial. The console works
            permanently and completely on its own, and the machine is explicitly designed to work
            alongside whatever app you already use rather than to sell you one.
          </p>
          <p>
            This is a genuine and increasingly unusual position. Our
            <a href="/best-treadmills-without-subscriptions/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">no-subscription roundup</a>
            works through what a gated console costs over five years, and the answer is frequently
            comparable to the price of the treadmill. A machine that asks for nothing after purchase
            has a materially different total cost from one that does not — the arithmetic in our
            <a href="/peloton-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Peloton guide</a>
            shows the gap clearly.
          </p>
          <p>
            What you give up is the integrated experience. There is no large screen, no instructor
            in front of you, and no automatic control of speed and incline from a class. If you run
            a third-party app on a tablet, you change the speed yourself when told — which, on this
            machine specifically, is a smaller inconvenience than usual, because changing speed is
            the thing it is good at.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The console is basic and that is deliberate.</strong> A backlit display, some
            onboard programmes, Bluetooth speakers and a shelf for your phone. Horizon spent the
            budget on the frame, the motor and the warranty instead. Whether that is the right trade
            depends entirely on whether a screen is what gets you training, which is the same
            question our
            <a href="/best-treadmills-without-subscriptions/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">subscription analysis</a>
            turns on. Some people need the class; those people should buy the machine with the
            class.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="in-use" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">4. How It Behaves in Use</h2>
<p>
            <strong>For interval work</strong>, it does what it claims. The dials are where your
            hands are, the response is quick, and sessions written as short efforts can be executed
            as written. This is the machine's reason to exist and it delivers on it.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>For steady running</strong>, it is unremarkable in the best sense. A 60-inch
            deck, a 3.0 CHP motor and a heavy frame is a combination that does not draw attention to
            itself, which is what you want on a long run.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>For walking</strong>, it is more machine than necessary. It works perfectly
            well, but almost everything you are paying for — deck length, motor headroom, fast speed
            changes — is irrelevant at 3 mph. A walker is better served by our
            <a href="/best-walking-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">walking picks</a>
            at considerably less money, including Horizon's own cheaper
            <a href="/horizon-t101-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">T101</a>.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>For incline work</strong>, 15% is ample and the incline motor is adequate. It is
            not instant in the way the speed response is, which is worth knowing if you were
            planning incline intervals specifically — see our
            <a href="/best-incline-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">incline guide</a>
            for what to look for there.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Noise</strong> is typical for a machine of this class: the motor is unobtrusive
            and footfall is the dominant sound, as it is on every treadmill. Anyone in a flat should
            read our
            <a href="/best-treadmills-for-apartments/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">apartment guide</a>
            before assuming any running treadmill is viable above a neighbour.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="folding-and-space" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">5. Folding, Weight and Space</h2>
<p>
            The deck folds with hydraulic assistance, which matters more than the folded dimensions
            do. Our
            <a href="/best-treadmills-for-small-spaces/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">small spaces guide</a>
            makes the point at length: a fold that requires you to take the deck's full weight stops
            happening by week three, and an assisted fold keeps happening indefinitely. This is the
            assisted kind, so the folded footprint is a number you can actually plan around.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>It is still heavy.</strong> Folding reclaims floor area; it does not make the
            machine portable. Moving it between rooms is a two-person job and moving it between
            floors is a planned event. Measure the delivery route — doorways, stairwell turns,
            ceiling height at the top of the stairs — before ordering, using the arithmetic in our
            <a href="/treadmill-dimensions-space-requirements/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">dimensions guide</a>.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Leave the run-off clearance.</strong> The space behind the deck is a safety
            requirement rather than spare floor, and a 60-inch deck plus adequate clearance is a
            real commitment in a small room. Check the unfolded footprint as well as the folded one,
            because on the days it is up, the unfolded number is your reality.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Ceiling height matters at incline.</strong> The deck sits some inches off the
            floor before any incline, and 15% adds more at the point where you stand. A basement
            with low headroom can make the top of the incline range unusable for a tall user.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who-should-buy" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">6. Who Should Buy It</h2>
<p>
            <strong>Buy it if you do structured sessions.</strong> Intervals, fartlek, tempo work
            with changes, anything where the speed moves during the session. This is the cheapest
            machine that does that properly and the gap over its competitors is real rather than
            marketing.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Buy it if you want deck length without a subscription.</strong> A 60-inch deck,
            a genuine 3.0 CHP motor, 325 lb capacity and a lifetime frame and motor warranty, with
            no account required, is a strong combination at this price. For a runner who already
            knows what they want to do, it is close to ideal.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Buy it if you liked a discontinued Johnson-built machine.</strong> Horizon is
            the same manufacturer as Matrix and the old
            <a href="/livestrong-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">LiveStrong</a>
            line, and this is the natural step up from a mid-range machine of that era.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Do not buy it if you want a screen and classes.</strong> The console is
            deliberately plain. If guided content is what gets you on the belt, buy a machine built
            around that and accept the subscription — our
            <a href="/peloton-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Peloton guide</a>
            and <a href="/nordictrack/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">NordicTrack guide</a>
            cover that side.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Do not buy it if you only walk.</strong> You are paying for capability you will
            not use. The <a href="/horizon-t101-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">T101</a>
            is the same manufacturer's answer at a much lower price.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Do not buy it if the room cannot take an unfolded 60-inch deck plus
            clearance.</strong> Assisted folding helps, but the footprint is what it is.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="ownership" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">7. Owning One</h2>
<p>
            <strong>The warranty shape is worth reading carefully.</strong> Horizon publishes
            lifetime cover on the frame and motor, which is uncommon and genuinely valuable, with
            shorter terms on parts and labour. The pattern is the same as the
            <a href="/horizon-t101-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">T101</a>: the
            lifetime element covers what rarely fails, and the wear items — belt, deck, rollers,
            console — are covered for a defined period after which they are your cost. Confirm the
            current terms before buying; they change between model years.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>That makes maintenance the thing that decides ownership cost.</strong> Belt and
            deck life is governed almost entirely by lubrication, and a machine used for interval
            work accumulates belt hours quickly. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-belt-lubrication/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">lubrication guide</a>
            covers the method and our
            <a href="/how-often-to-lubricate-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">interval guide</a>
            covers the schedule. Ten minutes every few months is the difference between a deck
            lasting five years and lasting two.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Interval work is harder on belt tracking</strong> than steady running, because
            repeated acceleration and deceleration nudges the belt off centre faster. Check tracking
            more often than you would otherwise, and correct it early —
            <a href="/how-to-center-treadmill-belt/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">centring</a>
            takes minutes when caught early and causes belt edge damage when left.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Vacuum the motor compartment</strong> a couple of times a year. Our
            <a href="/how-to-vacuum-treadmill-motor-compartment/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">guide</a>
            covers doing it safely, and it is the cheapest life extension available on any
            treadmill.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Total running cost is low.</strong> Lubricant, an eventual belt, and modest
            <a href="/treadmill-electricity-usage/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">electricity</a>.
            Our <a href="/treadmill-maintenance-cost/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">maintenance
            cost guide</a> has the real annual figures — this is tens of dollars a year, and the
            absence of a subscription is what makes that the whole story.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="alternatives" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">8. What to Cross-Shop</h2>
<p>
            <strong>Sole F63 and F65.</strong> The obvious comparison —
            <a href="/sole-fitness/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Sole</a> builds heavy,
            plain, subscription-free machines with long warranties, and the
            <a href="/sole-f63-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">F63</a> and
            <a href="/sole-f65-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">F65</a> sit either
            side of this price. They are arguably better built and they do not have the dial
            controls. If you never change speed mid-session, that trade favours Sole; if you do, it
            does not.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>NordicTrack's comparable models.</strong> More screen, more content, iFit
            attached. A different proposition rather than a better or worse one — see our
            <a href="/nordictrack/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">brand guide</a>.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The Horizon T101, downward.</strong> If the honest use case is walking and light
            jogging, the <a href="/horizon-t101-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">T101</a>
            saves substantial money for the same manufacturer's approach.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>A used commercial machine.</strong> At this budget, our
            <a href="/new-vs-used-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">used market analysis</a>
            shows you can buy considerably more hardware secondhand. You lose the warranty and the
            dial controls, and you gain a heavier frame and a longer deck. For a runner who does not
            do intervals, that trade is worth considering seriously.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>


      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">9. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What makes the Horizon 7.0 AT good for interval training?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Two things working together. Dials sit on the handrails where your hands already are, so you can go straight to a target speed without hunting for a button or breaking stride. And the motor is tuned to reach the new speed quickly rather than ramping gently. Fast controls on a slow-ramping belt would solve nothing — it is the combination that lets a thirty-second effort actually be thirty seconds at the right pace.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does the Horizon 7.0 AT need a subscription?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. There is no membership, account or trial required, and the console works permanently on its own. The machine is designed to sit alongside whatever app you already use rather than to sell you one. What you give up is an integrated screen experience — there is no instructor and no automatic speed control from a class, so you change the speed yourself when told.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What size is the Horizon 7.0 AT deck?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Horizon publishes 60 by 20 inches. Sixty inches is the threshold at which a treadmill stops constraining an average-height runner's stride, and a good number of machines at this price stop at 55, so it is one of the stronger reasons to choose it. Taller runners should still check the stride arithmetic before committing.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Horizon 7.0 AT vs Sole F63 — which is better?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                They are close on hardware and differ in philosophy. Sole builds a heavier, plainer machine with a long warranty and no dial controls. The 7.0 AT matches it broadly on specification and adds fast speed changes. If your sessions involve changing pace repeatedly, the Horizon is clearly better for you. If you run at one speed and want the most solid frame for the money, the Sole is the stronger buy.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is the Horizon 7.0 AT good for walking?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It works perfectly well, but it is more machine than a walker needs and almost everything you are paying for — deck length, motor headroom, fast speed changes — is irrelevant at 3 mph. Horizon's own T101 does the walking job for considerably less money with the same subscription-free approach. Buy the 7.0 AT for running and intervals, not for walking.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does the Horizon 7.0 AT fold?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, with hydraulic assistance, which matters more than the folded dimensions. An assisted fold keeps happening indefinitely, whereas a fold requiring you to take the deck's full weight tends to stop happening within a few weeks. It is still a heavy machine — folding reclaims floor area rather than making it portable, and moving it between floors is a planned event.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Common Mistakes Buying a 7.0 AT</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">This machine is bought for the wrong reason more often than it is bought badly, and the distinction matters.</p>
          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying it for the dials and then never changing speed</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">The interval controls are the reason this machine costs what it does, and they are worth nothing to someone who sets 6 mph and runs for forty minutes. If that is your training, a heavier, plainer machine at the same price is a better buy. Be honest about whether your sessions actually involve changing pace before paying for the feature that does it well.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Comparing 3.0 CHP against a rival’s peak horsepower</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Continuous duty and peak are different measurements, and comparing one against the other reaches the wrong conclusion every time. A 3.0 continuous rating is a genuinely stronger motor than a 3.5 peak one. If a competing listing gives only a peak figure, the comparison is not available — and the omission tells you something on its own.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Planning around the folded footprint only</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Assisted folding means the machine genuinely does get folded, which makes the folded number usable for planning. It does not remove the unfolded footprint on the days it is up, and it does not remove the run-off clearance behind the deck, which is a safety requirement rather than spare floor. Measure both, plus ceiling height at full incline for a tall user.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping lubrication on a machine doing interval work</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Repeated acceleration loads the belt and deck harder than steady running does, and it nudges the belt off centre faster. That means lubrication and tracking checks matter more here than on a machine used for steady jogging, not less. Ten minutes every few months, plus an occasional tracking check, is the difference between a deck lasting five years and lasting two.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

